      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "In Utah\u2019s climate, a roof typically lasts [Highlight start] 15 to 70+ years [Highlight end] depending on the material\u2014asphalt shingles generally last 15 to 30 years, while metal and tile roofs last 40 to 100+ years . High-altitude UV rays, heavy snow, freeze-thaw cycles, and occasional hail often shorten manufacturer lifespans by about 15% to 25%.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "[Highlight start] 15 to 70+ years [Highlight end]"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1,
          2,
          3
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Lifespan by Material"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "3-Tab Asphalt Shingles: 15 to 20 years. Most vulnerable to UV wear, wind, and temperature swings.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              6,
              7,
              8
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Architectural Asphalt Shingles: 20 to 30 years. Thicker and more durable than 3-tab shingles, with designer or impact-rated options sometimes pushing near 30 years.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              4,
              6,
              8
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Metal Roofing: 40 to 70+ years. Standing seam and coated metal panels shed snow well and resist UV and freeze-thaw damage, making them common in mountain areas.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              3,
              5,
              6,
              9
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Clay or Concrete Tile: 50 to 100+ years. Very durable against sun and weather, though underlayment and flashing eventually need maintenance.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              1
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "What Shortens a Utah Roof's Lifespan"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Freeze-Thaw and Snow: Repeated melting and refreezing stresses roofing materials and causes curling or cracking."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "High-Altitude UV: Intense sunlight dries out asphalt oils and speeds up granule loss."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Poor Attic Ventilation: Trapped heat and moisture bake shingles from below and can lead to ice dams in winter."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Installation Quality: Improper nailing, flashing, or cold-weather installation can cut years off a roof's life.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              2,
              3,
              4,
              5,
              7,
              9,
              10
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
